WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:11.050
Five Mongol envoys knelt on the beach at Kamakura
and stretched out their necks.

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:16.109
Their time had come, and a clear target for
the executioner’s sword would at least ensure

00:00:16.109 --> 00:00:17.280
it was painless.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:24.140
They gazed out over the deep blue of the Pacific
Ocean, waves lapping slowly.

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:29.039
How they wished their last sight could have
been an Ocean of grass swaying softly in a

00:00:29.039 --> 00:00:30.599
steppe breeze.

00:00:30.599 --> 00:00:37.010
Their hearts were calm as the blades descended
upon their naked necks.

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:42.700
They would be revenged.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:47.430
This execution of ambassadors was how Hojo
Tokimune, the Shiken - head of the Shogun’s

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:52.680
government - and ruler of Japan, gave his
definitive answer to Kublai Khan, Emperor

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:57.400
of China, Son of Heaven, Great Khan of the
Mongols.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.489
He would not submit.

00:00:59.489 --> 00:01:00.670
Not now.

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:01.670
Not ever.

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:02.670
Mt.

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:08.830
Fuji’s shining peak loomed over the headless
Mongol warriors.

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:11.880
Soon after, Kublai sent another five men.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:13.840
This time there would be no negotiations.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:17.119
They came to announce one thing.

00:01:17.119 --> 00:01:18.579
Invasion.

00:01:18.579 --> 00:01:23.880
It would be the biggest seaborne attack the
world had ever seen.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:29.270
The new envoys met the same end, but were
not granted the honor of dying in Mount Fuji’s

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:30.509
blessed shadow.

00:01:30.509 --> 00:01:35.150
Their blood stained the western beaches where
their compatriots had tried to land in Kyushu

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:38.020
a year before.

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:41.450
The first invasion had come in November 1274.

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:47.130
Great Korean ships replete with Mongol battle
regalia and tipped with grotesque figureheads

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:53.080
- still-living Japanese islanders from the
outlying domains of Tsushima and Iki were

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:56.939
nailed, screaming hideously, to the prows.

00:01:56.939 --> 00:02:02.520
The armada, stretching to the horizon, obscured
the waters - and made land in Hakata Bay,

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:03.930
northern Kyushu.

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:09.259
Tight ranks of soldiers descended from the
ships into the water, and marched in formation,

00:02:09.259 --> 00:02:11.610
shields held high, up on to the beach.

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:15.319
This phalanx warfare was unfamiliar to the
defending samurai.

00:02:15.319 --> 00:02:17.330
They were confused.

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:19.310
When were they supposed to shout their challenge?

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:20.310
To whom?

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:22.780
How would they know if opponents were of appropriate
rank?

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:29.129
One of the defenders made up his mind, lifted
his great bow, took aim at a mounted officer,

00:02:29.129 --> 00:02:34.200
and let fly.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:43.440
Hojo Tokimune, insecure in his recent tenure,
presiding over a crumbling realm beset with

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:46.970
woes, lay in his concubine’s lap as she
stroked his long hair.

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:52.330
His realm was an ancient one, even if the
house of Tokimune were merely backwater warriors

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:56.310
turned recent usurpers of the strings of national
power.

00:02:56.310 --> 00:03:02.580
The Mongol threat focused his mind, the first
full-scale invasions his country had ever

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:03.640
known.

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:12.020
For the first time in his young life he wondered
- how had Japan got here?

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:16.290
Tokimune became drowsy, and slipped into a
light sleep.

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:23.380
He dreamed slowly, vividly, and fitfully of
a Japan, not now, as it was, but as it had

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:26.494
been, back into the mists 

00:03:26.494 --> 00:03:55.404
of forgotten time.

00:05:05.273 --> 00:05:10.259
Hereupon, regretting the errors in the old
words, and wishing to correct the misstatements

00:05:10.259 --> 00:05:15.880
in the former chronicles, Her Highness Empress
Regnant Genmei, on the 18th day of the 9th

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:20.770
moon of the 4th year of Wado (November 3,
711) commanded me, Yasumaro, to select and

00:05:20.770 --> 00:05:25.290
record the old words, and dutifully to lift
them up to Her.

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:32.290
... I, Yasumaro, with true trembling and true
fear, bow my head, bow my head.

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:39.050
Court Noble, Futo no Yasumaro, Upper Division,
First Class, Fifth Rank, Fifth Order of Merit,

00:05:39.050 --> 00:05:43.980
paused in his writing, shifted his knees ever
so slightly to render his kneeling position

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:49.289
a little more comfortable, dipped his fine
brush into the pitch-black ink, and started

00:05:49.289 --> 00:05:50.860
his great endeavor.

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:55.020
A work to last for all time.

00:05:55.020 --> 00:06:01.199
Yasumaro went on to render the fables, legends,
and vaguely understood myth of ancient times

00:06:01.199 --> 00:06:02.990
as fact.

00:06:02.990 --> 00:06:09.139
Creating a divine fable to legitimize, and
deify, the rule of his, very human, imperial

00:06:09.139 --> 00:06:15.260
masters, and the right of his people, the
Yamato, to dominate all the other tribes and

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:24.569
peoples of the land we now know as Japan.

00:06:24.569 --> 00:06:30.030
He described how Izanagi and Izanami first
created the islands, mountains, rivers, herbs

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:34.330
and trees with drops of water from a coral
spear.

00:06:34.330 --> 00:06:39.819
Then they begat the Lord of the universe the
Sun Goddess Amaterasu, the greatest of their

00:06:39.819 --> 00:06:40.819
divine children.

00:06:40.819 --> 00:06:45.819
She sent her Grandson, Ninigi no Mikoto to
Earth as the first Ruler of the Land.

00:06:45.819 --> 00:06:51.729
But it was not only Ningi who descended to
earth, his father - Susa-no-o, God of Storms,

00:06:51.729 --> 00:06:56.990
uncouth and brash, was banished from heaven
for his foul and evil behavior.

00:06:56.990 --> 00:07:01.830
While Susa-no-o’s people conquered and flourished
in Izumo, in the west of the island of Honshu,

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:05.669
Ningi’s people also prospered, and multiplied
in the south.

00:07:05.669 --> 00:07:10.539
Within three generations they had grown powerful
enough to thrust north to central Honshu,

00:07:10.539 --> 00:07:13.540
battling foes to establish a new power base.

00:07:13.540 --> 00:07:18.660
Its names were myriad, but most know it as
.... Yamatai.

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:22.190
Yamatai was ruled over by an Emperor, Jimmu.

00:07:22.190 --> 00:07:25.430
The first of a line that was to last forever.

00:07:29.215 --> 00:07:34.931
Of course, it was probably not quite as Yasumaro
told it.

00:07:34.931 --> 00:07:39.400
For Yasumaro to serve a deity, the Imperial
Line required divine blood.

00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:45.370
And so, Yasumaro wove the myriad myths of
the Yamato people into a solid story, and

00:07:45.370 --> 00:07:51.740
in doing so ‘discovered,’ a royal lineage
that stretched back to the Sun herself.

00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:56.979
The Amaterasu of his story was probably based
upon the legendary great 3rd century shaman

00:07:56.979 --> 00:07:59.199
queen Himiko.

00:07:59.199 --> 00:08:03.990
Susa-no-o,[a] her brother, and his fight with
her, may have represented a royal disagreement

00:08:03.990 --> 00:08:06.460
as much as a heavenly battle.

00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:10.870
His was a work of propaganda, akin to the
Roman Aeneid, which wove together vaguely

00:08:10.870 --> 00:08:16.300
remembered myth, heroic legend, and outright
fabrications to legitimize the rule of Augustus

00:08:16.300 --> 00:08:26.580
Caesar in Rome or The Secret History of the
Mongols, which did the same for Genghis Khan.

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:32.630
The original settlers of Japan, however, came
in a far more human guise.

00:08:32.630 --> 00:08:39.080
They first crossed land bridges from the Asian
mainland tens of thousands of years before,

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:43.930
and continued to arrive in small groups from
all directions for eons afterwards.

00:08:43.930 --> 00:08:49.250
The population was small, perhaps 160,000
at its height, and so these people, called

00:08:49.250 --> 00:08:53.410
Jomon after the rope patterns they left on
their pottery, are thought to have lived a

00:08:53.410 --> 00:08:57.540
life of hunting and gathering the plentiful
resources they found around them.

00:08:57.540 --> 00:09:02.420
They did not know of ... war ... until a new
people began to make landfall.

00:09:02.420 --> 00:09:08.070
These were the people whose myths Yasumaro
wrote as fact.

00:09:08.070 --> 00:09:12.580
The ‘many-fenced palace’ of the God of
Storms was copied across the islands by a

00:09:12.580 --> 00:09:18.690
new and acquisitive, ambitious people, who
coveted the land, and protected a newly engineered

00:09:18.690 --> 00:09:22.680
resource – the rice field.

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:28.230
These are known as the Yayoi peoples, and
Japan would never be the same again.

00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:33.620
The ancient Chinese Kingdom of Wu, where Shanghai
now stands, was believed by the ancient Japanese

00:09:33.620 --> 00:09:36.250
to be where their ancestors came from.

00:09:36.250 --> 00:09:40.050
Refugees from that kingdom, which was destroyed
around the time that the Yayoi are believed

00:09:40.050 --> 00:09:41.890
to have migrated to Japan.

00:09:41.890 --> 00:09:46.610
Descriptions of the Wu and the ‘Wo,’ as
the Japanese were originally known in Chinese,

00:09:46.610 --> 00:09:52.750
their tattoos and warlike nature certainly
seem to bear more than a passing resemblance.

00:09:52.750 --> 00:09:57.870
The archaeological and DNA record shows that
a large body of the Yayoi period Japanese

00:09:57.870 --> 00:10:03.830
came from the north, Siberia, through Mongolia
and Manchuria, down the Korean Peninsula and

00:10:03.830 --> 00:10:05.730
across the seas.

00:10:05.730 --> 00:10:10.980
Some modern research also suggests that material,
and cultural similarities with civilizations

00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:15.510
on Java and other parts of South East Asia
may exist.

00:10:15.510 --> 00:10:20.440
What is certain is that the Yayoi peoples
were not the final pre-Japanese history humans

00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:23.370
to migrate to the Japanese isles.

00:10:23.370 --> 00:10:28.940
Sometime in the first few centuries of the
Common Era, a time of turmoil and war on the

00:10:28.940 --> 00:10:32.930
continent, a massive wave of people came from
northern China, bringing with them material

00:10:32.930 --> 00:10:38.120
wealth, and knowledge to improve just about
every facet of human life on the islands.

00:10:38.120 --> 00:10:43.320
Some became nobles, had their names recorded,
and entered the chronicles.

00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:48.080
Place and family names connecting modern Japan
with these ancient settlers can be found to

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:54.860
this day, but the exact details of early Japan
can still be said to be lost in the linguistic

00:10:54.860 --> 00:10:57.680
and cultural mists of time.

00:10:57.680 --> 00:11:03.805
Which leaves us with the mystery of just who
are the Japanese?

00:11:07.770 --> 00:11:13.390
The country formerly had a man as ruler.

00:11:13.390 --> 00:11:18.430
For some seventy or eighty years after that
there were disturbances and warfare.

00:11:18.430 --> 00:11:21.420
Thereupon the people agreed upon a woman for
their ruler.

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:23.740
Her name was Himiko.

00:11:23.740 --> 00:11:27.680
She occupied herself with magic and sorcery,
bewitching the people.

00:11:27.680 --> 00:11:30.410
Though mature in age, she remained unmarried.

00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:33.490
She had a younger brother who assisted her
in ruling the country.

00:11:33.490 --> 00:11:36.790
After she became the ruler, there were few
who saw her.

00:11:36.790 --> 00:11:41.010
She had one thousand women as attendants,
but only one man.

00:11:41.010 --> 00:11:46.630
She resided in a palace surrounded by towers
and stockades, with armed guards in a state

00:11:46.630 --> 00:11:48.110
of constant vigilance.

00:11:48.110 --> 00:11:53.940
When Himiko passed away, a great mound was
raised, more than a hundred paces in diameter.

00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:59.839
Over a hundred male and female attendants
followed her to the grave.

00:11:59.839 --> 00:12:05.270
Kofun.
162,000 of these burial mounds have been identified

00:12:05.270 --> 00:12:06.600
across Japan.

00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:12.620
They vary in shape and size, but the ‘classic’
kofun is shaped like a keyhole, or bell.

00:12:12.620 --> 00:12:15.810
The longest is over 400 metres.

00:12:15.810 --> 00:12:17.940
The burial chambers are of stone.

00:12:17.940 --> 00:12:22.750
Un-looted ones have been found to be adorned
with paintings of court life and filled with

00:12:22.750 --> 00:12:25.330
useful accessories for the afterlife.

00:12:25.330 --> 00:12:30.690
The pre-historic, semi-legendary age recorded
in the Chinese classic, Sanguo Zhi, 'Record

00:12:30.690 --> 00:12:35.500
of the Three Kingdoms,' not only told of the
enthronement of Queen Himiko, her spinning

00:12:35.500 --> 00:12:40.510
of spells to pacify the realm, and an influx
of people from lands over the seas.

00:12:40.510 --> 00:12:49.940
It saw the dawn of these massive megalithic
engineering projects.

00:12:49.940 --> 00:12:54.779
Despite her pure white robe of mulberry fiber,
the warm evening, and the flaming torches,

00:12:54.779 --> 00:13:00.480
a shiver of pure ice rent the girl’s heart
as she saw the mound which would be her resting

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:01.480
place.

00:13:01.480 --> 00:13:05.870
But she steeled herself, the magatama charms
which adorned her body rattling while she

00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:10.949
danced towards her destiny as a heavenly lady
in waiting to her deceased mistress, Ruler

00:13:10.949 --> 00:13:16.079
of Yamatai, Queen of Wa, Friend of Wei Dynasty
China.

00:13:16.079 --> 00:13:17.260
Himiko.

00:13:17.260 --> 00:13:21.420
Despite her fear and misgivings, the young
girl’s heart brimmed with pride to fulfill

00:13:21.420 --> 00:13:22.440
this duty.

00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:26.680
There never would be another such as her dead
Queen.

00:13:26.680 --> 00:13:31.029
Himiko had woven a spell over Yamatai, with
kido magic.

00:13:31.029 --> 00:13:35.699
The sorcery had wrought peace where once there
had been war, prosperity where once there

00:13:35.699 --> 00:13:37.100
had been none.

00:13:37.100 --> 00:13:42.230
The people loved her, yet none could set their
eyes upon her, even the 1,000 maidens who

00:13:42.230 --> 00:13:45.230
served her were ignorant of her face.

00:13:45.230 --> 00:13:49.740
The Queen would admit only onfe person to
her presence, her brother.

00:13:49.740 --> 00:13:54.740
This man served her food and wine, and communicated
laws and judgements to her flock.

00:13:54.740 --> 00:13:59.800
Himiko’s most astonishing achievement had
been sending four diplomatic missions to far-off

00:13:59.800 --> 00:14:05.430
China with gifts of slaves, and the highly
prized fine, strong cloth for which the Queen’s

00:14:05.430 --> 00:14:07.529
land was justly famous.

00:14:07.529 --> 00:14:12.370
In return, treasures, the likes of which had
never been seen before.

00:14:12.370 --> 00:14:18.009
Beads, bronze mirrors, and most prized of
all, swords, military banners and an official

00:14:18.009 --> 00:14:24.550
seal pronouncing her ‘Friend and Ally of
the Wei Dynasty’.

00:14:24.550 --> 00:14:29.560
Himiko and her chief envoys were symbolically
appointed to the highest ranks in the Wei

00:14:29.560 --> 00:14:31.080
Chinese military.

00:14:31.080 --> 00:14:35.960
Her magic had extended across the oceans,
and bewitched the Chinese ruler into giving

00:14:35.960 --> 00:14:38.490
her his greatest honour.

00:14:38.490 --> 00:14:45.360
With China’s recognition, all bowed before
her, and Yamatai came to dominate the Japanese

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:47.149
islands.

00:14:47.149 --> 00:14:56.950
But, as is the way of things, the Great Queen
breathed her last, and the people of Yamatai

00:14:56.950 --> 00:15:01.250
raised a great mound, a kofun, over her burial
chamber.

00:15:01.250 --> 00:15:07.980
100 youths and maidens were selected to serve
her until the end of time, and the young girl

00:15:07.980 --> 00:15:12.000
shivering in the summer evening heat was one.

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:18.250
She danced on past the crackling flames into
the kofun mound, and on ... to the eternal

00:15:18.250 --> 00:15:20.310
afterlife.

00:15:20.310 --> 00:15:25.630
Himiko and her serving-maidens are believed
to be at eternal peace at the Hashihaka kofun

00:15:25.630 --> 00:15:27.720
in Nara Prefecture.

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:37.380
“Assassination and murder followed; more
than one thousand were thus slain.

00:15:37.380 --> 00:15:43.630
A relative of Himiko named Iyo, a girl of
thirteen, was then made queen and order was

00:15:43.630 --> 00:15:44.630
restored.

00:15:44.630 --> 00:15:50.670
(Zhang) Zheng, an ambassador from Wei, issued
a proclamation to the effect that Iyo was

00:15:50.670 --> 00:15:51.670
the ruler.”

00:15:52.670 --> 00:15:54.810
This set a pattern in Japan.

00:15:54.810 --> 00:16:00.139
Throughout ancient times, women’s status
was high, and female monarchs repeatedly enter

00:16:00.139 --> 00:16:01.820
the chronicles.

00:16:01.820 --> 00:16:07.550
Some like Empress Jingu, beat the drums of
war, others seem to have followed Himiko and

00:16:07.550 --> 00:16:17.850
Iyo in fostering a land at peace with itself.

00:16:17.850 --> 00:16:22.839
This doctrine is amongst all doctrines the
most excellent, but it is hard to explain

00:16:22.839 --> 00:16:24.910
and hard to comprehend.

00:16:24.910 --> 00:16:29.560
Even the Duke of Zhou and Confucius could
not attain a knowledge of it.

00:16:29.560 --> 00:16:35.210
This doctrine can create religious merit and
retribution without measure and without bounds,

00:16:35.210 --> 00:16:39.310
and so lead on to a full appreciation of the
highest wisdom.

00:16:39.310 --> 00:16:47.870
Every prayer is fulfilled and naught is wanting.

00:16:47.870 --> 00:16:51.990
The people who made the Japanese islands their
home did not forget about the lands their

00:16:51.990 --> 00:16:56.920
ancestors had come from, nor about the Gods
of those lands, which they brought with them

00:16:56.920 --> 00:17:01.360
to become the Gods of the religion we now
know as Shinto.

00:17:01.360 --> 00:17:06.860
Long before the myths of Amaterasu and her
descendants were written, Shinto was a living

00:17:06.860 --> 00:17:09.740
breathing religion of nature spirits.

00:17:09.740 --> 00:17:13.189
Mountains, rivers, trees, stones, fire, water.

00:17:13.189 --> 00:17:17.689
But, the small kingdoms of the Japanese isles
had remained intimately connected to those

00:17:17.689 --> 00:17:29.740
on the Korean Peninsula, trading, exchanging,
learning, marrying, and warring.

00:17:29.740 --> 00:17:35.260
Embassies were exchanged, noble and royal
marriage alliances forged, technology transferred,

00:17:35.260 --> 00:17:36.970
and missionaries sent.

00:17:36.970 --> 00:17:41.170
There was a relationship of deep respect,
and so in the middle of the 6th century, when

00:17:41.170 --> 00:17:46.140
King Seong of Baekje, a kingdom in the western
part of the peninsula, sent his letter sharing

00:17:46.140 --> 00:17:51.600
the peace of the Buddha with Emperor Kinmei,
his Imperial highness’s chief advisor, Soga

00:17:51.600 --> 00:17:56.100
no Iname, stroked his beard and smiled behind
a ceremonial fan, a recently imported fad

00:17:56.100 --> 00:17:57.100
from Korea.

00:17:57.100 --> 00:18:01.290
His time had come at last.

00:18:01.290 --> 00:18:06.520
The long years of scheming with his Korean
kin and continental-minded allies in the Yamato

00:18:06.520 --> 00:18:10.040
court had resulted at last in this mission’s
arrival.

00:18:10.040 --> 00:18:15.049
It seemed sudden, heaven-sent, but it was
nothing of the sort.

00:18:15.049 --> 00:18:19.549
Soga and his family had spent years, perhaps
even decades, arranging it all.

00:18:19.549 --> 00:18:23.740
This was not the first time Buddha’s teachings
had reached Japan, a number of the common

00:18:23.740 --> 00:18:29.020
people had been preached to by monks in generations
past, some of the holy men even claimed to

00:18:29.020 --> 00:18:32.020
have come from as far away as the land of
the Buddha’s birth.

00:18:32.020 --> 00:18:38.080
But such common bare-foot migrant priests
received no patronage, and even less aristocratic

00:18:38.080 --> 00:18:39.080
interest.

00:18:39.080 --> 00:18:44.960
Soga no Iname’s family had been aware, perhaps
even secret followers, of the teachings for

00:18:44.960 --> 00:18:45.980
generations.

00:18:45.980 --> 00:18:51.650
He knew both that Buddhism was salvation for
his sovereign, and a way of concentrating

00:18:51.650 --> 00:18:54.030
power in his own hands.

00:18:54.030 --> 00:18:58.030
He would be the first noble of consequence
to take up Buddha’s banner.

00:18:58.030 --> 00:19:04.440
He and his clan would hold it aloft in battle,
claiming the divine power to smite and confound

00:19:04.440 --> 00:19:10.200
their foes.

00:19:10.200 --> 00:19:15.550
But nature, or maybe the old Shinto gods,
it seemed, did not agree with Soga’s new

00:19:15.550 --> 00:19:16.730
religion.

00:19:16.730 --> 00:19:21.250
Plague broke out, and Soga’s enemies at
court seized upon this proof that the true

00:19:21.250 --> 00:19:23.440
gods of the land were insulted.

00:19:23.440 --> 00:19:29.260
Emperor Kinmei, in a fearful rage and in mortal
fear for his life and kingdom, ordered Buddhist

00:19:29.260 --> 00:19:31.350
temples destroyed.

00:19:31.350 --> 00:19:38.390
It would be left to Soga no Iname’s descendants
to reclaim the Buddhist mandate.

00:19:38.390 --> 00:19:42.520
The army of the Imperial Princes and the troops
of the Ministers were timid and afraid, and

00:19:42.520 --> 00:19:44.140
fell back three times.

00:19:44.140 --> 00:19:49.750
At this the Imperial Prince [Shotoku], his
hair being tied on the temples, followed in

00:19:49.750 --> 00:19:51.250
the rear of the army.

00:19:51.250 --> 00:19:57.080
He pondered in his own mind, saying to himself:
“Without prayer we cannot succeed.”

00:19:57.080 --> 00:20:03.150
So he cut down a tree, and swiftly fashioned
images of the Four Heavenly Kings.

00:20:03.150 --> 00:20:05.660
Placing them on his top-knot, he uttered a
vow:

00:20:05.660 --> 00:20:11.230
“If we are now made to gain the victory
over the enemy, I promise faithfully to honor

00:20:11.230 --> 00:20:16.670
the four Heavenly Kings, guardians of the
world, by erecting to them a temple with a

00:20:16.670 --> 00:20:19.890
pagoda.”

00:20:19.890 --> 00:20:23.860
And so, Prince Shotoku swept to victory.

00:20:23.860 --> 00:20:28.070
When he was appointed head of government under
his aunt, Japan’s first and longest reigning

00:20:28.070 --> 00:20:33.630
female Empress Regnant, Suiko, he made Buddhism
the state religion.

00:20:33.630 --> 00:20:38.789
Together they established temples, sent embassies
to Sui China, established a calendar to understand

00:20:38.789 --> 00:20:44.500
the heavens, and a constitution to enshrine
both Buddhism and Chinese Confucian order

00:20:44.500 --> 00:20:49.510
as the guiding principles of life – at least
for aristocrats - in the new nation - which

00:20:49.510 --> 00:20:55.400
within a century would become generally known
by a term he is said to have invented, ‘The

00:20:55.400 --> 00:20:58.550
Land of the Rising Sun.’

00:20:58.550 --> 00:21:03.860
Nihon, or, Japan.

00:21:03.860 --> 00:21:08.980
From the chaos and tribal infighting of the
fifth century, Shotoku and Suiko established

00:21:08.980 --> 00:21:14.180
the roots of a stable state, which would in
time come to dominate the three main islands,

00:21:14.180 --> 00:21:20.430
Honshu, Kyushu and Shikoku, and impose semi-vassal
status on wild tribes of the north, and the

00:21:20.430 --> 00:21:24.220
tattooed Amami island peoples of the far south.

00:21:24.220 --> 00:21:28.470
Future religious and secular leaders would
claim that he came to them in visions and

00:21:28.470 --> 00:21:33.830
dreams, inspiring them to great deeds and
profound spiritual understanding.

00:21:33.830 --> 00:21:38.530
In his vision, his accomplishments, and the
recognition granted him down the ages, Prince

00:21:38.530 --> 00:21:46.610
Shotoku can justly claim to be the Father
of the Nation we now know as Japan.

00:21:52.880 --> 00:21:59.850
The farm boy stood in a simple tunic, rusty
knife in his belt, a tall spear at his side.

00:21:59.850 --> 00:22:01.720
He was feeling strong.

00:22:01.720 --> 00:22:05.309
The gathering cold of late autumn did not
bother him one bit.

00:22:05.309 --> 00:22:09.400
Instead the blue sky gave him hope and strength.

00:22:09.400 --> 00:22:13.150
The conscript reflected on the fact that he
was a peasant no more.

00:22:13.150 --> 00:22:17.380
He was now a great warrior for his lord and
king.

00:22:17.380 --> 00:22:22.429
He watched excitedly as the eagerly anticipated
great flotilla of ships entered the mouth

00:22:22.429 --> 00:22:26.350
of the Baengma River, on the East coast of
Korea.

00:22:26.350 --> 00:22:31.180
Tens of thousands of men bent to the oars,
chanting in unison as the small vessels, which

00:22:31.180 --> 00:22:36.809
had successfully made the treacherous crossing
from Kyushu, made their way up the river.

00:22:36.809 --> 00:22:42.880
Astride each ship, stood proud warriors, clan
banners held high, swords punching the air

00:22:42.880 --> 00:22:45.320
amid deafening cheers.

00:22:45.320 --> 00:22:46.820
Drums rang out.

00:22:46.820 --> 00:22:51.800
They were late in the season, and had been
lucky not to get caught in the autumn storms,

00:22:51.800 --> 00:22:53.429
but it mattered not.

00:22:53.429 --> 00:22:55.540
They were now here.

00:22:55.540 --> 00:23:01.510
Baekje, the kingdom from which Buddha’s
peace had entered Japan, was an ancient Japanese

00:23:01.510 --> 00:23:07.120
ally on the Korean Peninsula, but had fallen
to an alliance of Tang China and another Korean

00:23:07.120 --> 00:23:09.900
kingdom, Silla, in 660.

00:23:09.900 --> 00:23:13.820
Following that defeat, those who could had
fled to Japan to plan their comeback with

00:23:13.820 --> 00:23:15.919
the help of the Japanese.

00:23:15.919 --> 00:23:20.450
This vast army and armada of boats was the
result.

00:23:20.450 --> 00:23:24.980
It was a moment of pure joy for the young
conscript as he stood on the riverbank, ready

00:23:24.980 --> 00:23:30.210
to welcome the Japanese reinforcements, and
the returning Baekje refugees, and above all

00:23:30.210 --> 00:23:33.830
his rightful lord, the new King Pungjang.

00:23:33.830 --> 00:23:39.980
The King would stamp the seal of peace on
their lives, and coat it with the blood of

00:23:39.980 --> 00:23:41.660
the wretched oppressors.

00:23:41.660 --> 00:23:42.830
The river was filling up.

00:23:42.830 --> 00:23:47.210
It had become almost impossible to see the
water flowing beneath the sleek vessels, they

00:23:47.210 --> 00:23:48.770
were so numerous.

00:23:48.770 --> 00:23:51.050
800 his commander had told him.

00:23:51.050 --> 00:23:56.270
All the conscript knew was that with such
a host, they were sure of victory.

00:23:56.270 --> 00:23:58.170
Everyone was in good spirits.

00:23:58.170 --> 00:24:02.130
The moment for revenge had arrived.

00:24:02.130 --> 00:24:05.260
It was October 4th 663.

00:24:05.260 --> 00:24:11.850
The motherland was on the cusp of being restored
to its former glory.

00:24:11.850 --> 00:24:15.990
The flotilla got larger and larger as more
and more ships entered the estuary and started

00:24:15.990 --> 00:24:17.360
to head upstream.

00:24:17.360 --> 00:24:23.159
But there came a point when they were so jammed
together, that movement all but ceased.

00:24:23.159 --> 00:24:29.760
This was the moment the hidden Tang Chinese
ships had been waiting for.

00:24:29.760 --> 00:24:35.899
170 of them sprung the trap, speeding downriver
from inland harbors, raining flaming arrows

00:24:35.899 --> 00:24:39.820
down on the unprepared Japanese and Baekje
Korean vessels.

00:24:39.820 --> 00:24:45.020
The young conscript watched in horror as his
kingdom’s salvation floundered and died

00:24:45.020 --> 00:24:46.690
before his eyes.

00:24:46.690 --> 00:24:52.150
Then from the hills behind him came the sound
of shouts, a conch shell rang high, then low,

00:24:52.150 --> 00:24:56.020
... echoed eerily through the valley – the
order for cavalry to form up.

00:24:56.020 --> 00:24:57.020
Hwarang!

00:24:57.020 --> 00:24:59.250
Silla’s renowned cavalry.

00:24:59.250 --> 00:25:03.649
Trained by warrior monks from childhood to
revere the law, obey all orders, to love and

00:25:03.649 --> 00:25:08.080
fight to the death for their comrades, and
to exterminate their foes.

00:25:08.080 --> 00:25:12.050
They were said to sooner die than retreat.

00:25:12.050 --> 00:25:16.720
The young Baekje conscript now saw himself
for what he was.

00:25:16.720 --> 00:25:17.789
No great warrior.

00:25:17.789 --> 00:25:23.930
A mere mountain peasant dressed in hemp clothing,
armed only with a simple spear.

00:25:23.930 --> 00:25:29.830
It was now he knew fear in his heart.

00:25:29.830 --> 00:25:34.620
Behind Baekje’s dwindling forces, the Tang
ships had closed in, and hand-to-hand fighting

00:25:34.620 --> 00:25:37.410
covered the floating battlefield.

00:25:37.410 --> 00:25:41.620
Surviving Japanese warriors from sunken ships
climbed the muddy river banks, and joined

00:25:41.620 --> 00:25:46.549
the rear of the conscript’s formation, there
was nowhere to run from the circling Hwarang,

00:25:46.549 --> 00:25:50.550
the only hope was the ever-smaller mass of
men around their king.

00:25:50.550 --> 00:25:53.009
Then they broke.

00:25:53.009 --> 00:25:57.860
The few remaining men headed for the safety
of the hills as the Hwarang swarmed around

00:25:57.860 --> 00:26:01.520
them, impaling the fleeing runners on razor-sharp
lances.

00:26:01.520 --> 00:26:07.100
They held out, fighting a low-key guerilla
war for years in the mountains.

00:26:07.100 --> 00:26:12.480
But after a decade, the dwindling band of
freedom fighters knew that all was truly lost,

00:26:12.480 --> 00:26:19.509
there was to be no Baekje resurrection.

00:26:19.509 --> 00:26:24.600
Japanese ships again made the dangerous voyage
to the continental peninsula to perform the

00:26:24.600 --> 00:26:28.510
evacuation of their Korean allies and kin.

00:26:28.510 --> 00:26:33.890
The Minister Yeo Jasin, the Minister Gwisil
Jipsa, and others, men and women, to the number

00:26:33.890 --> 00:26:40.179
of over 700 persons, were removed and settled
in the district of Kamafu in the province

00:26:40.179 --> 00:26:41.539
of Omi.

00:26:41.539 --> 00:26:47.240
As Baekje fell, all those who could, perhaps
many thousands in all, escaped to the Japanese

00:26:47.240 --> 00:26:51.980
islands with the retreating Japanese troops,
and the decades following, they were welcomed,

00:26:51.980 --> 00:26:56.100
and integrated into society and national political
systems.

00:26:56.100 --> 00:27:02.940
Their traces survive to this day in place
names, culture, and family surnames.

00:27:02.940 --> 00:27:06.500
This was a pivotal moment for Japan.

00:27:06.500 --> 00:27:09.900
No longer would it be intimately entwined
with the continent.

00:27:09.900 --> 00:27:16.020
From now, it would turn inwards, concentrating
on its own affairs, staying well away from

00:27:16.020 --> 00:27:18.690
continental politics and conflicts.

00:27:18.690 --> 00:27:24.179
Silla, who soon conquered the whole peninsula,
never forgot that the Japanese had supported

00:27:24.179 --> 00:27:26.070
their Baekje enemies.

00:27:26.070 --> 00:27:32.190
The relationship between the Japanese isles
and the Koreas had soured, never to regain

00:27:32.190 --> 00:27:34.958
its former warmth.

00:27:41.549 --> 00:27:46.730
In ancient times, the kings of the Yin Dynasty
restored their country after transferring

00:27:46.730 --> 00:27:49.070
the capital five times.

00:27:49.070 --> 00:27:53.269
The sovereigns of the Zhou Dynasty ensured
peace in their country after establishing

00:27:53.269 --> 00:27:55.730
the capital three times.

00:27:55.730 --> 00:28:00.910
Today, as for the site of Heijo-kyo, the layout
of the four animals is in accord with the

00:28:00.910 --> 00:28:06.920
model, mountains guard the site in three directions,
and the divinations suggest good fortune.

00:28:06.920 --> 00:28:10.310
We should build a new capital at this site.

00:28:10.310 --> 00:28:14.870
Materials for construction, as the need arises,
should be listed and reported.

00:28:14.870 --> 00:28:15.870
Construction of streets and bridges should
be started after the autumn harvest so that

00:28:15.870 --> 00:28:16.870
the people who are willing to render services
would not be disturbed.

00:28:16.870 --> 00:28:19.830
Plans should be carefully worked out so that
they would not be changed subsequently.

00:28:19.830 --> 00:28:26.289
Bodhisena had traveled the world, over icebound
mountains, through parched desert, across

00:28:26.289 --> 00:28:34.140
lush farmland, and upon the deepest seas in
his quest to find Manjusri, a long-dead enlightened

00:28:34.140 --> 00:28:39.490
one, a Bodhisattva of wisdom, who had appeared
to him in a dream as a youth in India.

00:28:39.490 --> 00:28:45.270
It had taken him years, but now he was reaching
his journey’s end.

00:28:45.270 --> 00:28:51.870
Gyoki, the Japanese monk who walked beside
him WAS Manjusri reincarnated - he was sure

00:28:51.870 --> 00:28:52.870
of it.

00:28:52.870 --> 00:28:57.680
They had met before in another time and place
– another life – in the presence of the

00:28:57.680 --> 00:28:58.799
Buddha himself.

00:28:58.799 --> 00:29:08.460
Gyoki solemnly led Bodhisena on to his, their,
destiny, the great city of Heijo-Kyo, Japan’s

00:29:08.460 --> 00:29:10.050
new capital city.

00:29:10.050 --> 00:29:13.429
Nothing like this had been seen before.

00:29:13.429 --> 00:29:19.260
The land had been unified, no longer was it
merely the statelet of Yamato, it was now

00:29:19.260 --> 00:29:23.390
The Land of the Rising Sun, Japan.

00:29:23.390 --> 00:29:28.100
It was no longer appropriate or dignified
for the Court to up sticks and be loaded on

00:29:28.100 --> 00:29:31.640
to the back of ox wagons every few years.

00:29:31.640 --> 00:29:34.230
China had long ago built an established capital.

00:29:34.230 --> 00:29:39.010
It was high time that Japan should join the
civilized world in this matter, just as it

00:29:39.010 --> 00:29:42.549
had so many other ways over the last centuries.

00:29:42.549 --> 00:29:48.000
Foreign states needed to understand the full
dignity and civilization of the city’s inhabitants

00:29:48.000 --> 00:29:49.350
and its rulers.

00:29:49.350 --> 00:29:52.900
Needed to know where to send diplomatic missions,
and tribute.

00:29:52.900 --> 00:29:59.980
What is more, Heaven needed to know where
Human Power lay on earth - moving had surely

00:29:59.980 --> 00:30:04.330
confused the gods, caused them to look less
favorably on the realm.

00:30:04.330 --> 00:30:05.899
The time had come.

00:30:05.899 --> 00:30:11.350
On the day of Jia-Zi in the eleventh month,
the cabinet offered a proposal to the emperor

00:30:11.350 --> 00:30:16.679
as follows; We hear that, in ancient times,
lives were so simple that people lived in

00:30:16.679 --> 00:30:20.020
caves in winter and nests in summer.

00:30:20.020 --> 00:30:23.940
In recent years, the noble people live in
palaces instead.

00:30:23.940 --> 00:30:27.740
We also have the capital for the residence
of the emperor.

00:30:27.740 --> 00:30:32.451
Since the capital is visited by people from
remote provinces and foreign countries, how

00:30:32.451 --> 00:30:37.550
can we express the virtue of the emperor if
the capital lacks magnificence?

00:30:37.550 --> 00:30:43.010
We offer a proposal to decree that the authorities
should instruct noblemen and wealthy commoners

00:30:43.010 --> 00:30:48.980
to equip their houses with tiled roofs, vermilion
pillars and white walls.

00:30:48.980 --> 00:30:54.720
Heijo-Kyo was the answer to all these problems,
desires, and prayers.

00:30:54.720 --> 00:30:56.950
A city of 100,000.

00:30:56.950 --> 00:31:02.820
Nobles, commoners, and slaves alike lived
within its numerous precincts.

00:31:02.820 --> 00:31:06.950
Peasants delivered food and other supplies
from the hinterland, pretty girls arrived

00:31:06.950 --> 00:31:12.110
to make their fortune in the pleasure districts,
criminals slunk by in the shadows, hoping

00:31:12.110 --> 00:31:16.600
to grab a tiny slither of the glorious riches
for themselves.

00:31:16.600 --> 00:31:21.660
Imperial councilors and civil servants also
came from much further afield - from as far

00:31:21.660 --> 00:31:28.169
away as Persia, a land lost in the haze of
the vast sand and grass desert which was known

00:31:28.169 --> 00:31:30.200
to exist beyond China.

00:31:30.200 --> 00:31:35.190
Chinese scholars, merchants, engineers and
architects from Great Tang roamed the streets

00:31:35.190 --> 00:31:40.320
and haunted the halls of learning in their
flowing robes, sporting their long status

00:31:40.320 --> 00:31:46.370
marking nails - work with one’s hands and
fingers was left to mere illiterate peasants

00:31:46.370 --> 00:31:49.380
and the servile classes.

00:31:49.380 --> 00:31:55.419
A grand Imperial repository called Shosoin
was established to exemplify the permanence

00:31:55.419 --> 00:31:57.029
of the new city.

00:31:57.029 --> 00:32:02.110
The Royal Treasures, tribute and gifts from
neighboring states, some emanating from further

00:32:02.110 --> 00:32:07.650
even than the most exotic of inhabitants - the
extreme wilds of lands in the far west whose

00:32:07.650 --> 00:32:12.200
names were unknown - would no longer be subject
to rude upheaval.

00:32:12.200 --> 00:32:19.080
They would rest in peace, as symbols of Imperial
legitimacy, international recognition, and

00:32:19.080 --> 00:32:25.250
Power, in the sacrosanct precincts of the
brand-new Todaiji Temple.

00:32:25.250 --> 00:32:30.730
Prince Shotoku’s reforms had woven their
logical path to this point.

00:32:30.730 --> 00:32:36.250
The loss of allies and bases on the continent,
no longer a buffer zone, had led to this point.

00:32:36.250 --> 00:32:42.750
Japan’s establishment as a state had led
to this point.

00:32:42.750 --> 00:32:48.130
Of all the people who roamed the wide boulevards
of the new capital, Bodhisena truly stood

00:32:48.130 --> 00:32:49.130
out.

00:32:49.130 --> 00:32:53.730
He was by far the darkest-skinned, and perhaps
close to one of the most learned men to have

00:32:53.730 --> 00:32:57.299
ever walked the winding mountain roads of
Japan.

00:32:57.299 --> 00:33:02.000
A Tamil man from the deep south of what is
now southern India, he had traveled the world

00:33:02.000 --> 00:33:06.789
seeking Buddhist truth, and his search had
eventually ended here.

00:33:06.789 --> 00:33:10.059
He was only in his twenties.

00:33:10.059 --> 00:33:14.970
The Imperial court was thrilled to have a
guest from lands so close to those of Buddha’s

00:33:14.970 --> 00:33:20.010
birth, and granted Bodhisena land for a temple,
encouraging him to share his deep knowledge

00:33:20.010 --> 00:33:21.640
and saintly aura.

00:33:21.640 --> 00:33:26.570
However, Bodhisena did not simply settle for
a quiet temple life.

00:33:26.570 --> 00:33:33.159
He was granted the honour of putting the final
touch to the greatest devotional work of construction

00:33:33.159 --> 00:33:39.040
that Japan, and most of the world, had ever
seen.

00:33:39.040 --> 00:33:45.700
In 741, Emperor Shomu, desiring to show the
magnificence of his state, demonstrate the

00:33:45.700 --> 00:33:49.400
protection of the Lochana Buddha’s universal
order, and having heard that Tang China had

00:33:49.400 --> 00:33:55.600
already completed such a project, ordered
the erecting of a Great Buddha statue in Heijo-kyo.

00:33:55.600 --> 00:34:02.540
It was to be the center piece of a great institution
of religion and learning, 16 meters high,

00:34:02.540 --> 00:34:10.629
500 tons, cast entirely of bronze, and housed
in a great wooden temple hall, 50 by 86 meters

00:34:10.629 --> 00:34:15.580
with 84 massive cypress pillars – Todaiji.

00:34:15.580 --> 00:34:20.410
The world had never seen the like in wood
before – nor would ever again.

00:34:20.410 --> 00:34:27.810
Half of the population, around 2,600,000 people
donated, food, money or labour to the cause,

00:34:27.810 --> 00:34:32.750
and specialist craftsmen hired from throughout
the known world toiled for over a decade to

00:34:32.750 --> 00:34:35.849
realize the Imperial Dream.

00:34:35.849 --> 00:34:40.580
In 752, it was time for the final act.

00:34:40.580 --> 00:34:48.630
The eye-opening ceremony – to dedicate the
statue, to awaken the living Buddha within.

00:34:48.630 --> 00:34:54.700
Before dignitaries from Tang China, all the
Korean kingdoms, 10,000 guests, and 4,000

00:34:54.700 --> 00:35:01.130
dancers moving to the dignified and majestic
sounds of hichiriki oboes, fue flutes, koto,

00:35:01.130 --> 00:35:06.770
biwa lutes, and myriad drums which formed
the new Imperial Music brought by Bodhisena

00:35:06.770 --> 00:35:12.280
and Foche, the Tamil priest himself painted
the Buddha’s eyes.

00:35:12.280 --> 00:35:16.369
It must have been a profound moment of holiness
and spirituality.

00:35:16.369 --> 00:35:20.730
With the painting of his eye, the Buddha lived.

00:35:20.730 --> 00:35:22.230
In Nara.

00:35:22.230 --> 00:35:26.390
In Japan.

00:35:26.390 --> 00:35:31.350
The Great Buddha’s love and protection now
emanated from the Japanese capital city, to

00:35:31.350 --> 00:35:35.730
warm and protect the world with its light
and sanctity.

00:35:35.730 --> 00:35:40.230
The brush the Bodhisena used is still preserved
in the Shosoin treasury today.

00:35:40.230 --> 00:35:45.120
It took several decades for the luster to
wear off.

00:35:45.120 --> 00:35:49.820
For while the munificence of the great statue
undoubtedly protected the realm’s spiritual

00:35:49.820 --> 00:35:54.950
health, the financial and social cost took
a long-term toll.

00:35:54.950 --> 00:35:59.579
Furthermore, the great centers of learning
became great centers of Buddhist power and

00:35:59.579 --> 00:36:01.560
influence over the state.

00:36:01.560 --> 00:36:05.790
This was not how the new Emperor Kammu saw
his reign’s future when he ascended the

00:36:05.790 --> 00:36:11.950
throne in 781, and became determined to break
from this monster of religious control created

00:36:11.950 --> 00:36:13.000
by his predecessors.

00:36:13.000 --> 00:36:15.560
There was only one thing for it.

00:36:15.560 --> 00:36:20.079
The ‘eternal’ capital of Heijo-kyo would
have to be moved.

00:36:20.079 --> 00:36:25.710
The Buddhists could be left to twiddle their
thumbs and chant their sutras away from secular

00:36:25.710 --> 00:36:26.710
power.

00:36:26.710 --> 00:36:28.740
Nagaoka was the site chosen in 784.

00:36:28.740 --> 00:36:30.740
But it was not a success.

00:36:30.740 --> 00:36:37.630
A decade later, in 794, Heian-kyo was eventually
founded and over the next decades, blossomed

00:36:37.630 --> 00:36:44.180
into a glorious and fitting capital city to
truly rule the land in harmony.

00:36:44.180 --> 00:36:51.240
This new city grew in size with the building
of the vast sprawling mansions of the 1182

00:36:51.240 --> 00:36:56.700
noble families of the land, a third who traced
their lineage to Chinese and Korean families,

00:36:56.700 --> 00:37:02.930
a testament to the global draw of the polity
which had been built over the last two centuries

00:37:02.930 --> 00:37:04.560
in central Japan.

00:37:04.560 --> 00:37:10.770
The economy thrived, and the libraries became
the world’s envy, with over 1,500 Chinese

00:37:10.770 --> 00:37:17.010
classics available for the reference of ministers,
scholars, engineers, craftspeople and soothsayers.

00:37:17.010 --> 00:37:23.040
Over the next few hundred years, Chinese book
collectors even visited to copy volumes of

00:37:23.040 --> 00:37:27.400
ancient literature that had been lost in China
itself.

00:37:27.400 --> 00:37:33.210
Kyoto as it later became, was to remain the
Imperial Capital until 1869.

00:37:33.210 --> 00:37:39.712
But it was on Japan´s borders where the real
battles were being held.

00:37:47.589 --> 00:37:52.170
Emishi from the two provinces of Mutsu and
Echigo, their wild hearts as yet untamed,

00:37:52.170 --> 00:37:55.390
have repeatedly harmed Our imperial subjects.

00:37:55.390 --> 00:37:59.850
Accordingly, We have dispatched an officer
and have ordered the provinces to mobilize

00:37:59.850 --> 00:38:00.900
their regiments.

00:38:00.900 --> 00:38:07.490
They are to go forth... and punish the emishi.

00:38:07.490 --> 00:38:12.250
The villages of sturdy wooden huts were small,
but warm and secure.

00:38:12.250 --> 00:38:16.850
Nestled in the cozy confines of tree-bound
valleys, they were virtually hidden until

00:38:16.850 --> 00:38:18.770
you stumbled upon them.

00:38:18.770 --> 00:38:24.170
The villagers, called Emishi by their would-be
invaders from the south, led hard but hardy

00:38:24.170 --> 00:38:29.990
lives, hunting, trapping, gathering, and reaping
the grains of their half-wild paddy fields.

00:38:29.990 --> 00:38:35.040
Food was plentiful, the gods and spirits of
their northern mountains and forests provided

00:38:35.040 --> 00:38:38.510
abundant resources of every kind.

00:38:38.510 --> 00:38:44.040
And so why then should they bow to foreigners
- pay the fruits of their labor to men and

00:38:44.040 --> 00:38:46.339
women who lived in far-off palaces?

00:38:46.339 --> 00:38:50.400
What would the foreigners give them in exchange
for this?

00:38:50.400 --> 00:38:53.070
What more could they need?

00:38:53.070 --> 00:38:59.280
These people controlled their own destiny,
paid tribute to none, acknowledged no overlord

00:38:59.280 --> 00:39:01.740
but their own chiefs.

00:39:01.740 --> 00:39:05.750
Amongst the Eastern savages the emishi are
the most powerful, their men and women live

00:39:05.750 --> 00:39:10.079
together promiscuously, there is no distinction
of father and child.

00:39:10.079 --> 00:39:13.800
In winter they dwell in holes, in summer they
live in nests.

00:39:13.800 --> 00:39:18.099
Their clothing consists of furs, and they
drink blood…

00:39:18.099 --> 00:39:23.950
In ascending mountains they are like flying
birds; in going through the grass they are

00:39:23.950 --> 00:39:25.490
like fleet wolves.

00:39:25.490 --> 00:39:29.220
…
What we now think of as Japan, was not always

00:39:29.220 --> 00:39:30.220
Japan.

00:39:30.220 --> 00:39:34.670
In fact, the remote borders in the north,
the west and the south are still unfixed and

00:39:34.670 --> 00:39:36.480
disputed to this day.

00:39:36.480 --> 00:39:42.260
In the 8th and 9th century, the wild border
was far closer to the capital, so close in

00:39:42.260 --> 00:39:47.380
fact, that even the marches to the north of
what is now Tokyo were a hazy no man's land,

00:39:47.380 --> 00:39:53.920
a vague realm of independent and warlike peoples
known as Emishi.

00:39:53.920 --> 00:39:58.810
The ancient Japanese Court did not engage
in anthropological study to determine exactly

00:39:58.810 --> 00:40:06.890
who these people were, simply classing these
unsubjugated - free - peoples as barbarians.

00:40:06.890 --> 00:40:10.849
But modern research has led us to believe
that they were farmers and hunters of mixed

00:40:10.849 --> 00:40:15.490
ethnicity, composed of peoples similar to
those who had settled in the south, but perhaps

00:40:15.490 --> 00:40:20.450
with a stronger dose of blood from the people
we know now as the Ainu of modern-day Japan's

00:40:20.450 --> 00:40:22.230
extreme north.

00:40:22.230 --> 00:40:27.900
Periodically, tribes of these Emishi people
bowed to their powerful southern neighbours,

00:40:27.900 --> 00:40:33.830
begged admittance to the tax role, and were
resettled within the burgeoning Japanese imperium.

00:40:33.830 --> 00:40:38.740
Whether this resettlement resembled the relatively
peaceful settlement of Goths and other non-subject

00:40:38.740 --> 00:40:44.290
tribes as farmers in Roman territory, or represented
a more sinister ethnic cleansing is unclear,

00:40:44.290 --> 00:40:49.653
but it is thought at least some of the settlers
were captive women and children, who’s still

00:40:49.653 --> 00:40:55.250
resisting husbands and fathers were then enticed
to surrender and join them in exile.

00:40:55.250 --> 00:41:01.000
However, in the main, the north remained beyond
the pale, and the Court at Heijo-kyo decided

00:41:01.000 --> 00:41:07.730
that something must be done about it.

00:41:07.730 --> 00:41:11.880
In the early 8th century, several punitive
expeditions consisting mainly of infantry

00:41:11.880 --> 00:41:18.140
conscripts set out, but the skill of the enemy
horsemen, cumbersome supply lines and deep

00:41:18.140 --> 00:41:21.970
winter snows meant little was achieved.

00:41:21.970 --> 00:41:28.260
And so in 774, the Emperor decided to do something
more definitive.

00:41:28.260 --> 00:41:34.920
He declared the commencement of THE GREAT
PACIFICATION ERA.

00:41:34.920 --> 00:41:39.250
Because [military action] brings hardship
to the people, We have long valued the broad

00:41:39.250 --> 00:41:44.480
virtue that embraces the myriad things [and
have eschewed war].

00:41:44.480 --> 00:41:48.930
But a report from Our generals makes it clear
that the barbarians have not amended their

00:41:48.930 --> 00:41:50.420
wild hearts.

00:41:50.420 --> 00:41:55.730
They invade Our frontiers and ignore the instructions
of the Sovereign.

00:41:55.730 --> 00:42:01.760
What must be done cannot be avoided ....
The Heijo-kyo Court were conscripts, mainly

00:42:01.760 --> 00:42:05.040
from provinces adjoining the barbarian-territories.

00:42:05.040 --> 00:42:09.410
Non-professional peasant serving as part of
their tax duty, providing their own weapons

00:42:09.410 --> 00:42:13.550
and armour and not expecting to be in the
field for long.

00:42:13.550 --> 00:42:19.530
Those who commanded them were chosen for their
noble titles as much as their leadership prowess.

00:42:19.530 --> 00:42:26.329
The Emishi, whose own recorded testimony is
lost to time, seem to have been warriors born,

00:42:26.329 --> 00:42:31.970
and despite their numerical and equipmental
inferiority, could as with the Viet Min in

00:42:31.970 --> 00:42:38.609
Vietnam, attack swiftly from nowhere, before
melting back into the dense, dark, forests

00:42:38.609 --> 00:42:40.070
of their homeland.

00:42:40.070 --> 00:42:46.100
While the huge Yamato armies did score some
wins, in the main, they stumbled around in

00:42:46.100 --> 00:42:51.020
territory they did not know, losing men to
ambush, getting hungrier and hungrier as their

00:42:51.020 --> 00:42:54.109
supply lines grew ever longer.

00:42:54.109 --> 00:42:58.760
An age old story.

00:42:58.760 --> 00:43:05.079
The Emishi chieftain, Aterui sat atop his
fleet horse, his bow as yet unstrung.

00:43:05.079 --> 00:43:08.260
It was the fifth month of 789.

00:43:08.260 --> 00:43:12.780
He gazed at the vast enemy force arrayed before
him in the valley.

00:43:12.780 --> 00:43:18.319
Behind the marching Yamato troops sent by
Emperor Kammu, smoked 800 of his people's

00:43:18.319 --> 00:43:19.500
homes.

00:43:19.500 --> 00:43:24.880
Razed as the slow moving infantry progressed
towards their doom.

00:43:24.880 --> 00:43:32.339
Because the flaming homes had been sacrificed
to a grand plan.

00:43:32.339 --> 00:43:36.770
This advance guard had been lured over the
Koromo River by easy pickings, and the promise

00:43:36.770 --> 00:43:42.570
of a seemingly small and easily defeatable
Emishi skirmish force of a mere few hundred.

00:43:42.570 --> 00:43:44.690
The Emishi stood their ground at first.

00:43:44.690 --> 00:43:48.750
Let the attackers think it was the main force,
earnestly defending their homes.

00:43:48.750 --> 00:43:53.740
They then feigned retreat, withdrawing while
keeping up a running battle with the 4,000

00:43:53.740 --> 00:43:54.960
Yamato troops.

00:43:54.960 --> 00:43:59.890
The invader's plan was for the remainder of
the massive invasion force, nearly 50,000

00:43:59.890 --> 00:44:07.510
men, to cross the river now, with no Emishi
defenders to threaten and thwart their passage.

00:44:07.510 --> 00:44:10.190
Now was Aterui's moment.

00:44:10.190 --> 00:44:14.180
He and his thousand warriors sounded their
battle cry.

00:44:14.180 --> 00:44:18.869
Blood-curdling roars echoed through the hills
and thickly treed valleys, magnifying their

00:44:18.869 --> 00:44:20.730
volume to a storm.

00:44:20.730 --> 00:44:26.110
The tramping army below them on the valley
floor hesitated, a sharp intake of breath

00:44:26.110 --> 00:44:32.670
sounded, and a proud Chinese-style war banner
drooped slightly as its bearer looked around,

00:44:32.670 --> 00:44:36.040
spooked and afraid.

00:44:36.040 --> 00:44:38.250
Aterui sounded his conch horn.

00:44:38.250 --> 00:44:42.000
It rang through the valleys and hills of his
homeland.

00:44:42.000 --> 00:44:46.640
The cavalry started slowly down the forested
mountain, as yet the enemy could not see them,

00:44:46.640 --> 00:44:51.510
they could simply hear the cries and shouts
of a large host approaching fast.

00:44:51.510 --> 00:44:54.930
The men on the valley floor’s imaginations
ran wild.

00:44:54.930 --> 00:44:59.030
It seemed as if a great horde of riders was
bearing down upon them.

00:44:59.030 --> 00:45:01.349
It was too much for the peasant levies.

00:45:01.349 --> 00:45:03.619
They broke and ran.

00:45:03.619 --> 00:45:08.230
Trampling their once proud war banners beneath
their freezing, sandled, feet.

00:45:08.230 --> 00:45:10.750
Those that managed to reach the river began
to wade outwards.

00:45:10.750 --> 00:45:14.770
Towards the safety of the far bank, and the
troops yet to cross.

00:45:14.770 --> 00:45:16.980
But these were treacherous waters.

00:45:16.980 --> 00:45:18.930
They flowed fast, and furious.

00:45:18.930 --> 00:45:22.250
Over 1,000 men succumbed to the depths.

00:45:22.250 --> 00:45:26.140
1,250 more survived only by discarding their
weapons.

00:45:26.140 --> 00:45:30.960
A sacrifice to the goddess of this forsaken
northern waterway.

00:45:30.960 --> 00:45:34.420
The army did not attempt another crossing.

00:45:34.420 --> 00:45:38.650
The commander, Ki no Kosami, issued his report
to the emperor:

00:45:38.650 --> 00:45:44.940
My staff and I have discussed this and concluded
that our best course is to disband the army,

00:45:44.940 --> 00:45:48.829
return the provisions, and prepare for emergencies....

00:45:48.829 --> 00:45:55.900
Thus we have sent orders to the various armies
to disband and withdraw.

00:45:55.900 --> 00:46:00.530
More campaigns followed, but in the face of
mounting opposition among the population,

00:46:00.530 --> 00:46:03.869
it was clear that something had to change.

00:46:03.869 --> 00:46:08.690
And so, after the death of Emperor Kammu,
who had invested so much time and energy in

00:46:08.690 --> 00:46:14.240
this GREAT PACIFICATION, the Court came up
with a cunning plan.

00:46:14.240 --> 00:46:18.320
In 811, victory was declared.

00:46:18.320 --> 00:46:21.339
Nothing had changed.

00:46:21.339 --> 00:46:26.010
The Emishi simply continued their wild and
free ways in the way they saw fit.

00:46:26.010 --> 00:46:32.650
It would be another 400 years before the north
was fully integrated into Japan, and it would

00:46:32.650 --> 00:46:36.420
be cultural assimilation rather than conquest.

00:46:36.420 --> 00:46:42.910
However, for now a conch shell of freedom
rang through the mountains and valleys of

00:46:42.910 --> 00:46:47.395
the Emishi’s northern homeland.

00:46:50.610 --> 00:46:58.520
A western houri beckons with her white hand
Inviting the stranger to intoxicate himself

00:46:58.520 --> 00:47:03.059
with her white hand
That western houri with features like a flower

00:47:03.059 --> 00:47:07.680
She stands by the wine warmer, and laughs
with the breath of spring

00:47:07.680 --> 00:47:12.320
Laughs with the breath of spring,
Dances with a dress of gauze!

00:47:12.320 --> 00:47:19.880
“Will you be going somewhere, milord, now,
before you are drunk?”

00:47:19.880 --> 00:47:24.809
The young embassy member, Abe no Nakamaro,
had caught a glimpse of the girl with the

00:47:24.809 --> 00:47:30.910
golden hair in the crowd as the Japanese embassy
paraded into the Tang Chinese capital of Chang-an

00:47:30.910 --> 00:47:36.130
through the great eastern gate of the city
and proceeded to the diplomatic quarters where

00:47:36.130 --> 00:47:40.089
they would be accommodated during their stay
in the Chinese capital.

00:47:40.089 --> 00:47:46.460
It had been a grueling odyssey from Heijo-kyo
in that year of 717.

00:47:46.460 --> 00:47:50.609
The thought of setting sail from the port
of Naniwa, with his mother’s farewell poem

00:47:50.609 --> 00:47:57.920
ringing in his ears, brought tears even now,
thousands of miles away at journey’s end.

00:47:57.920 --> 00:48:03.280
Of the four ships which set sail, only two
had made it across the raging seas south of

00:48:03.280 --> 00:48:04.280
Kyushu.

00:48:04.280 --> 00:48:05.280
Island hopping until the specs of land ran
out and the ships were forced to brave the

00:48:05.280 --> 00:48:06.280
great expanse of ocean before China appeared
on the horizon as a grey haze.

00:48:06.280 --> 00:48:09.640
He knew that former missions had sailed a
safer route via Korea, but Baekje’s fall

00:48:09.640 --> 00:48:12.050
and Silla’s belligerence had put a stop
to that.

00:48:12.050 --> 00:48:13.050
Now the young diplomat, only 19, had seen
Chinese ships, he knew he would never set

00:48:13.050 --> 00:48:14.050
foot in a Japanese one again.

00:48:14.050 --> 00:48:15.050
He also swore that should he survive to return
home, he would inform his superiors in no

00:48:15.050 --> 00:48:16.050
uncertain terms that Japanese shipbuilding
was a skill that required more refinement.

00:48:16.050 --> 00:48:19.650
Once in China though, a thing of true wonder
had revealed itself - the road and relay station

00:48:19.650 --> 00:48:24.770
network which stretched unhindered to the
farthest borders of the realm with standardized

00:48:24.770 --> 00:48:27.360
systems and in great order.

00:48:27.360 --> 00:48:33.160
In China, all roads lead to the capital Chang-an!

00:48:33.160 --> 00:48:37.770
The embassy had ridden in official carriages,
as guests of state, crossing great rivers

00:48:37.770 --> 00:48:43.190
on stone bridges or regular, river-worthy
ferries, staying each night in comfortable

00:48:43.190 --> 00:48:48.819
relay stations, and contemplating the vastness
of the countryside as the oxen plodded their

00:48:48.819 --> 00:48:51.290
weary way on the hard road.

00:48:51.290 --> 00:48:57.930
And now the young embassy member was here
in the centre of the world, a city of more

00:48:57.930 --> 00:49:03.770
than one million from all corners of the earth,
inside the awe-inspiring fortifications...

00:49:03.770 --> 00:49:09.720
and all he could think of was the revealing
dress of gauze, the curve of the body revealed

00:49:09.720 --> 00:49:17.109
beneath, and the outlandish yet oh-so-enticing
hair, like fine golden strands woven through

00:49:17.109 --> 00:49:19.119
silken cloth.

00:49:19.119 --> 00:49:24.350
He wondered what type of barbarian she was,
whether she had come from the end of the world

00:49:24.350 --> 00:49:25.350
as he had?

00:49:25.350 --> 00:49:26.350
Although, if that was so, it must be a different
end, because no one like her graced his homeland.

00:49:26.350 --> 00:49:27.350
This truly was a city of wonders, where all
the peoples of the world gathered to bask

00:49:27.350 --> 00:49:28.350
in the Chinese emperor, the Son of Heaven’s,
divine effervescence.

00:49:28.350 --> 00:49:30.720
He knew he would be spending months, perhaps
years as a guest of the government, in special

00:49:30.720 --> 00:49:35.780
facilities, afforded little movement, a gilded
confinement.

00:49:35.780 --> 00:49:38.760
But he would somehow evade the guard.

00:49:38.760 --> 00:49:45.619
To stroke those long golden locks, and discover
what lay beneath the gauze dress.

00:49:45.619 --> 00:49:49.850
This truly was a city of wonders.

00:49:49.850 --> 00:49:56.680
He would discover the World in Chang-an, and
return to Heijo-kyo to recount stories of

00:49:56.680 --> 00:49:58.609
his epic adventures.

00:49:58.609 --> 00:50:06.510
Of that he was certain.

00:50:06.510 --> 00:50:12.130
Between 607 and 839AD, 21 missions were dispatched
to China.

00:50:12.130 --> 00:50:17.470
The first mission infuriated the Chinese Emperor,
and nearly led to war, by insinuating that

00:50:17.470 --> 00:50:19.619
the two nations were equal.

00:50:19.619 --> 00:50:25.290
"The heavenly sovereign of the east respectfully
addresses the emperor of the west."

00:50:25.290 --> 00:50:28.380
東天皇敬白西皇帝
However, his forces were indisposed at the

00:50:28.380 --> 00:50:33.109
time, engaged in trying to dominate the northern
Korean Kingdom of Goguryo, and he settled

00:50:33.109 --> 00:50:38.930
for a polite, but haughty, letter to be delivered,
to place the mere 'sovereign' of this barbarian

00:50:38.930 --> 00:50:43.319
nation, below himself, the 'Son of Heaven'

00:50:43.319 --> 00:50:45.569
The Emperor greets the sovereign of Yamato.

00:50:45.569 --> 00:50:50.579
Your chief envoy, has come and stated in detail
your good intentions.

00:50:50.579 --> 00:50:55.450
We have been graced with reception of the
heavenly mandate to rule the universe and

00:50:55.450 --> 00:50:59.720
will extend the influence of our virtue to
all beings.

00:50:59.720 --> 00:51:05.790
In our concern to nurture and edify the people,
we do not distinguish between near and far.

00:51:05.790 --> 00:51:10.910
I have heard that the sovereign resides beyond
the sea and treats his people with benevolence,

00:51:10.910 --> 00:51:15.980
that peace reigns within his country, that
people's customs are harmonious, and that

00:51:15.980 --> 00:51:18.869
they are of an honest nature.

00:51:18.869 --> 00:51:22.550
You have come from afar to bring tribute.

00:51:22.550 --> 00:51:28.829
We are delighted with this splendid demonstration
of your sincerity.

00:51:28.829 --> 00:51:34.319
The chief envoy, Ono no omi Imoko, reported
that Sui China was “an admirable country,

00:51:34.319 --> 00:51:38.650
whose laws are complete and fixed,” and
set the scene for the next two centuries of

00:51:38.650 --> 00:51:45.369
exchange, during which Japan acquired knowledge
of religion, laws, technology, cuisine, agriculture,

00:51:45.369 --> 00:51:51.670
industry, culture, fine Chinese manufactures,
and perhaps most importantly, respect on the

00:51:51.670 --> 00:51:55.329
world stage and senior rank in the hierarchy
of nations.

00:51:55.329 --> 00:52:01.369
It, however, had to grit its teeth and accept
the barbarian status which the Chinese bestowed.

00:52:01.369 --> 00:52:06.540
The ship he was attempting to return home
aboard ran aground in Annam, modern-day northern

00:52:06.540 --> 00:52:13.540
Vietnam, and Abe no Nakamuro never did return
home, dying in Chang-an aged 72 in 770.

00:52:13.540 --> 00:52:19.290
To this day, he remains deeply respected,
a symbol of peace and mutual friendship, in

00:52:19.290 --> 00:52:23.030
both China and Japan.

00:52:23.030 --> 00:52:28.859
When I look up into the vast sky tonight,
is it the same moon that I saw rising from

00:52:28.859 --> 00:52:29.859
behind Mt.

00:52:29.859 --> 00:52:45.010
Mikasa at Kasuga Shrine
all those years ago?

00:52:45.010 --> 00:52:48.440
In the breeze
Scatter soft snow flurries

00:52:48.440 --> 00:52:51.190
So brief that
Its intermittent

00:52:51.190 --> 00:52:55.130
Fall is sad, indeed…

00:52:55.130 --> 00:53:00.780
It was the end of 10th century, a time of
weak central rule and chaos in the countryside.

00:53:00.780 --> 00:53:05.850
Not only in Japan - for the past century or
more the entire world had been in turmoil.

00:53:05.850 --> 00:53:10.720
The Vikings were ravaging Europe, the Mayan
Kingdoms of central America were in swift

00:53:10.720 --> 00:53:15.970
decline, China was divided by conflict and
epidemics raged throughout the globe.

00:53:15.970 --> 00:53:20.680
Over the past centuries, conflict with Silla
on the Korean Peninsula and the danger of

00:53:20.680 --> 00:53:27.160
travel in war-torn China had meant that the
once glorious diplomatic missions ceased.

00:53:27.160 --> 00:53:32.540
Immigration similarly dried up, trade faltered,
and the all-important intellectual exchange

00:53:32.540 --> 00:53:37.460
on which earlier development had been based,
faded into distant memory.

00:53:37.460 --> 00:53:46.890
The court lost control of the provinces, and
retreated within itself.

00:53:46.890 --> 00:53:51.720
Despite this calamity, however, a great flowering
of culture was occurring in the, now almost

00:53:51.720 --> 00:53:55.780
ancient, two hundred-year-old capital, Kyoto.

00:53:55.780 --> 00:53:58.819
Great volumes of poetry were declaimed for
posterity.

00:53:58.819 --> 00:54:01.940
Gorgeous clothing recorded in immortal artworks.

00:54:01.940 --> 00:54:07.930
Music and dance of great sophistication composed
to be handed down the generations for a thousand

00:54:07.930 --> 00:54:09.119
years.

00:54:09.119 --> 00:54:13.770
Perhaps most wonderful of all, a great literary
tradition came into being.

00:54:13.770 --> 00:54:19.070
The world’s first ever novel, Murasaki Shikibu’s
Tale of Genji, was written in serial form

00:54:19.070 --> 00:54:23.850
in the early 11th century, and other women,
especially those of the court, brushed their

00:54:23.850 --> 00:54:30.069
witticisms and ironic reflections into diaries
which are still enjoyed to this day.

00:54:30.069 --> 00:54:34.950
Prime among these female authors, somewhat
akin to trend-setting celebrity influencers

00:54:34.950 --> 00:54:40.390
of our modern world, was Sei Shōnagon
Fine then!

00:54:40.390 --> 00:54:43.270
How to be hard-hearted, from me
You have learned!

00:54:43.270 --> 00:54:48.260
But to promise, then not come-
Who taught you that, I wonder!

00:54:48.260 --> 00:54:55.310
As far as Sei was concerned, the common people
were little better than worms.

00:54:55.310 --> 00:54:59.369
One occasionally caught a glimpse of them
as one traveled through a village, their clothes

00:54:59.369 --> 00:55:06.559
threadbare, unperfumed, and untrammeled by
color of rank, their children naked and gaping.

00:55:06.559 --> 00:55:12.290
Once, she had seen a small boy, no doubt abandoned
by his commoner mother, much as animals leave

00:55:12.290 --> 00:55:18.309
their young to fend for themselves, standing
and gaping at her from the side of a road.

00:55:18.309 --> 00:55:24.430
He did not move, simply stared at her carriage
the whole time, gawping.

00:55:24.430 --> 00:55:26.300
Could anything have been more rude?

00:55:26.300 --> 00:55:33.119
Sei Shonagon, lady in waiting, court chronicler,
diarist and social commentator had reflected

00:55:33.119 --> 00:55:35.829
on this and other events.

00:55:35.829 --> 00:55:39.750
As far as the courtier was concerned, it all
went to show just why the court should be

00:55:39.750 --> 00:55:44.540
kept apart as far as possible from what lay
without.

00:55:44.540 --> 00:55:50.069
She had heard that the common people mated
without even attempting poetic courting first.

00:55:50.069 --> 00:55:54.880
The noble lady simply imagined it to be a
free for all in their hovels.

00:55:54.880 --> 00:55:59.099
The males did exhibit a modicum of decency
by living with their lover's parents and their

00:55:59.099 --> 00:56:03.299
begotten offspring, at least until he had
had enough and wandered elsewhere, or succumbed

00:56:03.299 --> 00:56:08.089
to one of the diseases which were of course
rampant among the servile masses.

00:56:08.089 --> 00:56:13.980
Perhaps most astonishingly, however, the females
fed offspring with milk from their own breasts,

00:56:13.980 --> 00:56:18.910
not even having the wherewithal to hire a
team of wet nurses to suckle their young.

00:56:18.910 --> 00:56:23.789
Sei herself, who had left a good-for-nothing
husband behind when she entered court, had

00:56:23.789 --> 00:56:29.319
a more refined approach to woo, or reject
her many lovers and courtiers.

00:56:29.319 --> 00:56:34.530
Exchanges of poetry, perfumed letters, half-feigned
modesty, well planned coquettishness, and

00:56:34.530 --> 00:56:39.069
the disposal and scorning of favours calculated
at just the right moment.

00:56:39.069 --> 00:56:46.349
But even Sei, the master of the game of love
did not always get it right

00:56:46.349 --> 00:56:48.200
To be abandoned
Is my fate

00:56:48.200 --> 00:56:51.480
I know so well;
Uncomprehending

00:56:51.480 --> 00:56:55.481
Do my tears fall.

00:57:05.180 --> 00:57:09.630
The razor-sharp sword slashed downwards towards
the unmounted Onda Hachiro Moroshige’s neck,

00:57:10.630 --> 00:57:16.869
To finish the job, the rider grabbed the unhelmeted
head by its hair and twisted, ripped the head

00:57:16.869 --> 00:57:18.869
from its body.

00:57:18.869 --> 00:57:23.880
The victorious killer threw away the prize
as if it were nothing, and galloped proudly

00:57:23.880 --> 00:57:24.880
away.

00:57:24.880 --> 00:57:30.520
As she rode off the field of war into the
distant mountains, Tomoe Gozen, removed her

00:57:30.520 --> 00:57:35.900
helmet, and shook her long jet-black hair
free, it billowed in the wind behind her like

00:57:35.900 --> 00:57:41.450
a battle standard, announcing to all that
one of history’s greatest women warriors

00:57:41.450 --> 00:57:45.630
had fought her last battle, and fought it
well.

00:57:45.630 --> 00:57:53.310
While Sei Shonagon and her noble kin lived
their closeted existence in Kyoto, over in

00:57:53.310 --> 00:57:58.339
the east events that would have repercussions
reaching to the modern-age were evolving.

00:57:58.339 --> 00:58:05.230
A class of clans, connected by hereditary
service to a military commander were emerging.

00:58:05.230 --> 00:58:11.980
As they served only one lord, they became
known by a word which means to serve.

00:58:11.980 --> 00:58:13.630
Samurai.

00:58:13.630 --> 00:58:18.090
Within 100 years, in the 11th century, at
about the same time as the age of chivalry

00:58:18.090 --> 00:58:23.650
was dawning in Europe, powerful samurai warrior
clans were exerting great control over the

00:58:23.650 --> 00:58:24.720
Imperial Court.

00:58:24.720 --> 00:58:30.950
One in particular, the Taira, seemed to be
in the ascendancy, and the leader of the clan:

00:58:30.950 --> 00:58:36.480
Kiyomori managed to have his three-year-old
grandson installed on the Chrysanthemum throne

00:58:36.480 --> 00:58:39.280
as Emperor Antoku.

00:58:39.280 --> 00:58:43.090
All seemed to be going very well.

00:58:43.090 --> 00:58:48.200
In the east, Taira’s ancient foe who had
been thought vanquished, the Minamoto clan

00:58:48.200 --> 00:58:51.760
lead by Yoritomo raised a host, and marched
west.

00:58:51.760 --> 00:58:56.680
When the Taira generals perceived their resurrected
foe’s strength, they retreated swiftly back

00:58:56.680 --> 00:58:59.520
to Kyoto without engaging.

00:58:59.520 --> 00:59:04.640
The next few years saw the east and north
fall to Minamoto forces, while the Taira lands

00:59:04.640 --> 00:59:08.000
suffered natural disaster, famine and pestilence.

00:59:08.000 --> 00:59:13.160
However, they remained undefeated and safe
in their Kyoto base, biding their time to

00:59:13.160 --> 00:59:14.839
strike.

00:59:14.839 --> 00:59:17.960
In 1184, the Taira felt their time had come.

00:59:17.960 --> 00:59:23.740
A huge force of 100,000 was levied, and the
men of the Capital region made a leisurely

00:59:23.740 --> 00:59:29.609
progress north to combat the Minamoto in their
mountain lairs.

00:59:29.609 --> 00:59:31.119
On June 2.

00:59:31.119 --> 00:59:34.859
1184, battle was joined at Kurikara.

00:59:34.859 --> 00:59:38.660
It took place in the old honorable samurai
fashion.

00:59:38.660 --> 00:59:43.700
Melees were fought between picked champions,
warriors called out their lineage before engaging,

00:59:43.700 --> 00:59:47.480
and all the proper protocols were observed.

00:59:47.480 --> 00:59:50.330
Until night fell.

00:59:50.330 --> 00:59:56.440
The heavily outnumbered Minamoto sent flaming
bulls, enraged and in mortal panic running

00:59:56.440 --> 00:59:58.000
through the Taira troops.

00:59:58.000 --> 01:00:01.799
In the chaos, the camp was abandoned, and
it is said

01:00:01.799 --> 01:00:07.559
Thus did seventy thousand horsemen of the
Taira perish, buried in this one deep valley;

01:00:07.559 --> 01:00:12.109
the mountain streams ran with their blood
and the mound of their corpses was like a

01:00:12.109 --> 01:00:14.340
small hill.

01:00:14.340 --> 01:00:19.940
The Taira fled Kyoto, taking the young Emperor
Antoku, his treasury, the Imperial regalia,

01:00:19.940 --> 01:00:22.319
and anything else they could grab.

01:00:22.319 --> 01:00:27.299
They fled still further west until they could
flee no more.

01:00:27.299 --> 01:00:30.690
Final battle was enjoined at a place called
Dannoura.

01:00:30.690 --> 01:00:36.859
The fighting took place with ships' decks
forming a treacherous, swaying battlefield.

01:00:36.859 --> 01:00:41.940
One wrong step, and heavily armored warriors,
unused to fighting on the sea, would fall

01:00:41.940 --> 01:00:43.160
to watery deaths.

01:00:43.160 --> 01:00:49.850
Emperor Antoku, 6 years old, was placed on
an insignificant looking boat, so that while

01:00:49.850 --> 01:00:55.049
the Minamoto concentrated their arrow fire
on the flag-ship, he remained safe.

01:00:55.049 --> 01:01:00.589
But a traitor, Taguchi Shigeyoshi, defected
and revealed the ruze.

01:01:00.589 --> 01:01:06.240
Arrows rained down on the rough-hewn boat
which formed the last few feet of Emperor

01:01:06.240 --> 01:01:09.359
Antoku's once glorious realm.

01:01:09.359 --> 01:01:14.890
As the Taira realized there was no further
to flee, the Emperor's grandmother held him

01:01:14.890 --> 01:01:17.079
in her arms.

01:01:17.079 --> 01:01:19.040
Finally, she jumped.

01:01:19.040 --> 01:01:30.000
"In the depths of the Ocean, we have a capital"
The Taira were destroyed for all time.

01:01:36.050 --> 01:01:41.579
Minamoto Yoritomo was granted the title of
Shogun, and his warrior-ruled samurai administration

01:01:41.579 --> 01:01:45.780
swiftly removed political power from Kyoto's
hands.

01:01:45.780 --> 01:01:51.640
Henceforth, true power would be wielded from
Kamakura in the east.

01:01:51.640 --> 01:01:58.190
Samurai power, largely based in the East at
Kamakura, and later Edo would last 700 years

01:01:58.190 --> 01:02:05.319
until 1868 when the last Shogun resigned,
and the Emperor also moved East from Kyoto

01:02:05.319 --> 01:02:10.000
to his new capital of Tokyo.

01:02:16.450 --> 01:02:21.490
Monks and priests today are fawning and devious,
and they confuse the people and lead them

01:02:21.490 --> 01:02:22.490
astray.

01:02:22.490 --> 01:02:27.309
Not a single person in the entire population
will possess a heart of goodness; there will

01:02:27.309 --> 01:02:33.869
be nothing but binding and enslaving, killing
and injuring, anger and contention.

01:02:33.869 --> 01:02:36.270
...
Pestilence will become rampant, comets will

01:02:36.270 --> 01:02:42.180
appear again and again, two suns will come
forth side by side, and eclipses will occur

01:02:42.180 --> 01:02:44.309
with unaccustomed frequency.

01:02:44.309 --> 01:02:50.060
Black arcs and white arcs will span the sky
as harbingers of ill fortune, stars will fall,

01:02:50.060 --> 01:02:54.279
the earth will shake, and noises will issue
from the wells.

01:02:54.279 --> 01:02:59.440
Torrential rains and violent winds will come
out of season, famine will constantly occur,

01:02:59.440 --> 01:03:02.640
and grains and fruits will not ripen.

01:03:02.640 --> 01:03:07.279
Marauders from many other regions will invade
and plunder the nation, the people will suffer

01:03:07.279 --> 01:03:15.680
all manner of pain and affliction, and no
place will exist where one may live in safety.

01:03:15.680 --> 01:03:22.920
The priest Nichiren placed down his pen, knelt
on the hard, earthen floor of his hut and

01:03:22.920 --> 01:03:28.699
gazed, through the open door, down his green,
pine-covered valley.

01:03:28.699 --> 01:03:32.819
He had said his piece.

01:03:32.819 --> 01:03:37.670
Rulers do not generally like to be told they
are evil and mistaken, especially young, rash

01:03:37.670 --> 01:03:44.270
ones who live in fear of invasions predicted
by raving holier-than-thou priests.

01:03:44.270 --> 01:03:50.460
Rival priests with the ruler’s ear, do not
like to be described as fawning and devious.

01:03:50.460 --> 01:03:55.599
And no one likes to think that marauders from
beyond the sea will appear over the horizon

01:03:55.599 --> 01:04:00.680
to slaughter the men, rape the women, and
enslave the children.

01:04:00.680 --> 01:04:06.690
Nichiren was condemned to death, and dragged
out of town to the execution grounds at Katase.

01:04:06.690 --> 01:04:11.299
In the dark of night, he frantically threw
his holy vestments on the branch of a tree

01:04:11.299 --> 01:04:14.960
to avoid them being soiled by his blood.

01:04:14.960 --> 01:04:19.539
The calm sea shimmered in the light of the
moon, and soft waves bathed the beach with

01:04:19.539 --> 01:04:21.839
their gentle ebb and flow.

01:04:21.839 --> 01:04:26.900
As the executioner’s sword hovered above
his neck, awaiting the order to strike Nichiren

01:04:26.900 --> 01:04:34.200
raised his face, and despite himself, he swiftly
composed a prayer to the God of the Moon.

01:04:34.200 --> 01:04:37.900
Suddenly, the sky was rent in two by a brilliant
light.

01:04:37.900 --> 01:04:44.020
Nichiren heard the dull yet sonorous sound
as the sword fell from his would-be killer’s

01:04:44.020 --> 01:04:48.770
grasp, and the patter of running feet on the
sandy beach.

01:04:48.770 --> 01:04:51.100
The execution corps fled.

01:04:51.100 --> 01:04:57.020
Soon, the holy man was alone with the sea,
the stars, ... and the moon.

01:04:57.020 --> 01:04:59.230
His saviour.

01:04:59.230 --> 01:05:06.100
The priest let two handfulls of sand seep
slowly through his fingers, got slowly to

01:05:06.100 --> 01:05:11.270
his feet, walked up to where the grass met
the sand, retrieved his vestments from the

01:05:11.270 --> 01:05:16.150
branch where they still hung, and then sank
to the ground once more.

01:05:16.150 --> 01:05:22.920
The divine being had vouched for his virtuosity,
saved him from death, ... to continue his

01:05:22.920 --> 01:05:27.199
mission on earth.

01:05:30.000 --> 01:05:35.640
And Nichiren was right: after the relative
stability of the Shogunate’s early years,

01:05:35.640 --> 01:05:40.940
environmental catastrophe, natural disasters,
and weakening government were leading to social

01:05:40.940 --> 01:05:47.661
breakdown, starvation - and appeared to be
ushering in the end of days.

01:05:47.661 --> 01:05:49.480
But that was nothing.

01:05:49.480 --> 01:05:55.549
As Nichren had predicted, the Horsemen of
the Apocalypse were about to descend on the

01:05:55.549 --> 01:05:58.240
Japanese isles.

01:05:58.240 --> 01:06:00.080
Mongols.

01:06:00.080 --> 01:06:05.380
We by the Grace and decree of Heaven, Emperor
of Great Mongolia, present a letter to the

01:06:05.380 --> 01:06:07.220
King of Japan.

01:06:07.220 --> 01:06:12.670
We have pondered that from ancient times,
even the princes of small states have striven

01:06:12.670 --> 01:06:16.640
to cultivate friendly intercourse with those
of adjoining territories.

01:06:16.640 --> 01:06:23.579
We beg that hereafter you, O King, will establish
friendly relations with us so that the sages

01:06:23.579 --> 01:06:26.880
may make the four seas their home.

01:06:26.880 --> 01:06:29.440
Is it reasonable to refuse intercourse with
each other?

01:06:29.440 --> 01:06:35.780
It will lead to war, and who is there who
likes such a state of things!

01:06:35.780 --> 01:06:40.110
Think on this, O King!

01:06:40.110 --> 01:06:44.420
The first letter had arrived in 1266.

01:06:44.420 --> 01:06:49.190
Ignoring these communications, and general
ignorance in Japan of just how powerful and

01:06:49.190 --> 01:06:54.990
large the Mongol Empire was – which stretched
from Manchuria to Poland – led to Mongol

01:06:54.990 --> 01:07:05.680
troops storming through the surf at Hakata
bay in 1274.

01:07:05.680 --> 01:07:07.859
The defender’s arrow flew sure.

01:07:07.859 --> 01:07:11.310
The Mongol toppled from his horse, and waves
closed around him.

01:07:11.310 --> 01:07:13.340
Then the enemy paused.

01:07:13.340 --> 01:07:19.829
As one, the men in the rear ranks raised strange
looking short bows, a great wave of arrows

01:07:19.829 --> 01:07:26.829
darkened the sky, shield-less as was their
way, the defending samurai fell in droves.

01:07:26.829 --> 01:07:30.190
Suddenly, thunder roared a short way off.

01:07:30.190 --> 01:07:35.109
Not in the clear, pale autumn sky, but on
the ground!

01:07:35.109 --> 01:07:37.230
Blood and flesh of samurai flew everywhere.

01:07:37.230 --> 01:07:39.940
Then the thunder pealed again, and again.

01:07:39.940 --> 01:07:44.020
These Mongol devils seemed somehow to have
enlisted the very heavens to fight on their

01:07:44.020 --> 01:07:45.020
side.

01:07:45.020 --> 01:07:47.730
The samurai turned and fled.

01:07:47.730 --> 01:07:53.180
They would fight men to the death, but dueling
with deities was a very different proposition.

01:07:53.180 --> 01:07:59.100
The enemy advanced up the wet beach slowly,
letting loose wave after wave of arrows, and

01:07:59.100 --> 01:08:01.790
propelling their grenade-like bombs asunder.

01:08:01.790 --> 01:08:06.130
The samurai continued a fighting retreat throughout
that terrible day.

01:08:06.130 --> 01:08:08.730
But the enemy came on.

01:08:08.730 --> 01:08:09.730
Unstoppable.

01:08:09.730 --> 01:08:10.730
Invincible.

01:08:10.730 --> 01:08:12.630
Burning, pillaging, killing.

01:08:15.315 --> 01:08:16.875
Night fell.

01:08:16.875 --> 01:08:23.739
And in the morning, they were gone.

01:08:23.739 --> 01:08:27.851
By the time news of the landing reached the
young shogun Hojo Tokimune on the other side

01:08:27.851 --> 01:08:33.130
of the country a heaven-sent typhoon had consigned
many of the enemy’s ships to the bottom

01:08:33.130 --> 01:08:38.779
of the ocean, and the rest scuttling for the
safety of Korean harbours.

01:08:38.779 --> 01:08:41.900
Japan had been lucky this time.

01:08:41.900 --> 01:08:46.190
No one knew why, but after that first day
of battle on Kyushu, the enemy had returned

01:08:46.190 --> 01:08:49.239
to their great ships and sailed away.

01:08:49.239 --> 01:08:53.160
Perhaps they were looking for an easier landing
place, maybe it had only been a reconnaissance

01:08:53.160 --> 01:08:54.160
mission.

01:08:54.160 --> 01:08:59.580
Either way, the typhoon had put paid to whatever
devilry they had kept in store.

01:08:59.580 --> 01:09:05.930
All knew they would return, and Shiken Hojo
Tokimune ordered a frenzy of defensive measures.

01:09:05.930 --> 01:09:11.110
All along the eastern coast walls were to
be built, look outs to be permanently posted,

01:09:11.110 --> 01:09:20.100
troop numbers multiplied, even weapons to
be redesigned to counter the new threat.

01:09:21.493 --> 01:09:26.130
Kublai smashed his fist into the floor.

01:09:26.130 --> 01:09:29.170
These snakes, those worms, these Dwarvish
Barbarians of the East.

01:09:29.170 --> 01:09:34.799
They had performed relatively well against
one puny reconnaissance mission in the previous

01:09:34.799 --> 01:09:40.240
year, and now they thought they could behead
his peace envoys with impunity!

01:09:40.240 --> 01:09:45.510
No action could be more heinous - greater
nations had been pulverised, entire cities

01:09:45.510 --> 01:09:48.569
put to the sword for less.

01:09:48.569 --> 01:09:50.480
They would pay dearly.

01:09:50.480 --> 01:09:54.710
A people should know when they have met their
match.

01:09:54.710 --> 01:09:59.650
Kublai’s generals, flush from having re-united
China under his rule with their recent victory

01:09:59.650 --> 01:10:04.739
over the southern provinces, now turned their
faces eastwards.

01:10:04.739 --> 01:10:09.409
Lacking knowledge of the sea, and remembering
the storms that had plagued the first exploratory

01:10:09.409 --> 01:10:16.860
expedition in 1274, they turned to two Yangtze
river pirates Zhu Qing and Zhang Xuan for

01:10:16.860 --> 01:10:18.350
their expertise.

01:10:18.350 --> 01:10:21.110
Two fleets would sail for Japan.

01:10:21.110 --> 01:10:27.510
One, of 900 ships, from Korea, and one of
3,500 ships from southern China.

01:10:27.510 --> 01:10:35.380
They would carry 142,000 men, the biggest
invasion force in history, not to be exceeded

01:10:35.380 --> 01:10:42.130
until the Allied invasions of Normandy in
1944, nearly 700 years later.

01:10:42.130 --> 01:10:45.380
Shiken Hojo Tokimune was petrified.

01:10:45.380 --> 01:10:49.890
He knew what his fate would be should the
Mongols reach his capital city of Kamakura.

01:10:49.890 --> 01:10:55.070
It was well known that the Mongols refused
to spill monarch’s blood, but Tokimune,

01:10:55.070 --> 01:10:58.920
although a ruler, was in truth merely a regent.

01:10:58.920 --> 01:11:01.860
Not of royal blood.

01:11:01.860 --> 01:11:05.390
His family had usurped both Emperor and Shogun.

01:11:05.390 --> 01:11:10.540
His end would not be swift and after his behaviour
towards the envoys, he knew the Mongols would

01:11:10.540 --> 01:11:15.440
enjoy a degree of creativity in their disposal
of him.

01:11:15.440 --> 01:11:18.630
Wild riders chased him down in his dreams.

01:11:18.630 --> 01:11:23.770
For once the young, hot-headed Shogunal regent
knew fear.

01:11:23.770 --> 01:11:29.990
To calm him, Tokimune’s spiritual counselor,
a recent refugee from southern China, the

01:11:29.990 --> 01:11:35.830
Zen Master, Mugaku Sogen, told him the story
of when the stinking barbaric nomads had reached

01:11:35.830 --> 01:11:38.270
his temple in China.

01:11:38.270 --> 01:11:43.510
Mugaku had knelt unmoving on the floor, the
only monk who had not fled.

01:11:43.510 --> 01:11:49.110
The flames of Mongol devastation crackled
around him as a lone rider approached, dismounted

01:11:49.110 --> 01:11:52.120
and drew his sword for the mortal blow.

01:11:52.120 --> 01:11:57.860
Mugaku, ignoring the stench of horse and human
sweat, raised his voice to a little over a

01:11:57.860 --> 01:12:02.050
whisper, and looked the rough-looking rider
in the eye:

01:12:02.050 --> 01:12:05.910
I searched the universe, and found the answer.

01:12:05.910 --> 01:12:10.090
People are empty, even Buddha’s teachings
a void.

01:12:10.090 --> 01:12:15.989
Your great sword,
will be as lightening cutting the spring breeze.

01:12:15.989 --> 01:12:22.940
The warrior paused, bowed low, and left the
monk to live.

01:12:22.940 --> 01:12:27.870
Mugaku had made his way eastwards to the land
where the Sun Rose, and Buddha’s Law was

01:12:27.870 --> 01:12:29.679
still revered.

01:12:29.679 --> 01:12:36.120
Quietly the aged Chinese monk advised the
young Japanese ruler to meditate, to find

01:12:36.120 --> 01:12:40.080
the source of his cowardice and fear within.

01:12:40.080 --> 01:12:45.510
Having done so, Tokimune screamed, “Katsu!”

01:12:45.510 --> 01:12:47.429
“VICTORY.”

01:12:47.429 --> 01:12:48.699
Mugaku smiled.

01:12:48.699 --> 01:12:55.280
“It is true that the son of a lion roars
as a lion!”

01:12:55.280 --> 01:13:01.600
Tokimune sent messengers to the Court to request
that all temples and shrines pray for victory.

01:13:01.600 --> 01:13:05.560
Japan was on high alert.

01:13:05.560 --> 01:13:14.720
Kikuchi Takefusa, mounted atop a fine steed,
bound in purple armor, crimson cape billowing

01:13:14.720 --> 01:13:20.600
in the fresh sea breeze watched the water
boil with enemy ships.

01:13:20.600 --> 01:13:26.199
600 years before, his family had been placed
on this border after the fall of Baekje to

01:13:26.199 --> 01:13:31.170
guard against invasion from what had once
been their Korean homeland.

01:13:31.170 --> 01:13:37.270
That attack had never come, and countless
generations had lived in peace.

01:13:37.270 --> 01:13:39.050
Until now.

01:13:39.050 --> 01:13:44.389
The Mongols had brought their Korean, Jurchen,
and Chinese vassals to subjugate and add yet

01:13:44.389 --> 01:13:46.719
another realm to their vast empire.

01:13:46.719 --> 01:13:50.600
Would they never be satisfied?

01:13:50.600 --> 01:13:55.429
The Korea-based fleet had ravaged the outer
islands of Tsushima and Iki again.

01:13:55.429 --> 01:14:01.030
And on June 21st 1281 the enemy had appeared
off the Kyushu horizon.

01:14:01.030 --> 01:14:03.630
The fighting was fierce in Hakata Bay.

01:14:03.630 --> 01:14:08.030
But all attempts at landing had been foiled
by the samurai forces.

01:14:08.030 --> 01:14:14.520
The great ships remained, threatening, massive,
darkening the ocean, awaiting the massive

01:14:14.520 --> 01:14:20.940
but severely delayed reinforcements from China
which would undoubtedly deal a mortal blow.

01:14:20.940 --> 01:14:23.460
The defenders did not sit idle.

01:14:23.460 --> 01:14:25.960
Small boats put out in the dead of each night.

01:14:25.960 --> 01:14:30.610
Mongol ships were boarded, their crews put
to the sword, vessels were fired and set loose

01:14:30.610 --> 01:14:31.900
to cause panic.

01:14:31.900 --> 01:14:34.260
A pandemic broke out.

01:14:34.260 --> 01:14:35.870
Thousands of the invaders perished.

01:14:35.870 --> 01:14:39.860
The ships started to rot.

01:14:39.860 --> 01:14:42.010
Then the China fleet arrived.

01:14:42.010 --> 01:14:45.130
Months late, but glorious in its great multitude.

01:14:45.130 --> 01:14:46.850
There was little hope for the defenders.

01:14:46.850 --> 01:14:49.320
They knew their time had come.

01:14:49.320 --> 01:14:55.239
The retired Emperor Kameyama sent an offering
to his divine ancestor, Amaterasu, in her

01:14:55.239 --> 01:15:01.260
chief shrine at Ise, imploring her to intervene
to save her children.

01:15:01.260 --> 01:15:08.030
That very same evening, a storm exploded from
the blue August skies.

01:15:08.030 --> 01:15:13.429
The wind roared, and the waves rose higher
than the enemy ships’ mastheads.

01:15:13.429 --> 01:15:20.340
Susa-no-o, God of Storms, was doing his sister
Amaterasu’s bidding at last.

01:15:20.340 --> 01:15:25.780
When the Susa-no-o had had enough, the Great
Khan and Son of Heaven’s vast army and armada

01:15:25.780 --> 01:15:31.510
were nothing more than throngs of forlorn
bodies, and an abundance of driftwood gentling

01:15:31.510 --> 01:15:39.739
lapping against the quiet Kyushu shores in
Amaterasu’s bright, soft, summer radiance.

01:15:39.739 --> 01:15:45.090
When the wicked invaders again arrived in
1281 and all persons, believing that the expulsion

01:15:45.090 --> 01:15:50.179
of the enemy could be effected only by divine
will and never by human power... reverently

01:15:50.179 --> 01:15:56.900
looked up to heaven, and a divine storm rose
in mighty force and scattered the enemy ships,

01:15:56.900 --> 01:16:05.040
and the enemy perished all at once.

01:16:05.040 --> 01:16:10.590
In China, The Mongols never totally recovered
from the massive material and morale seeping

01:16:10.590 --> 01:16:16.760
defeat, and although their Chinese domination
would struggle on until 1368, it had been

01:16:16.760 --> 01:16:20.310
in truth dealt a mortal blow.

01:16:20.310 --> 01:16:25.960
Against all the odds, Japan’s ancient, Mythical
Mother – Amaterasu, with a helping hand

01:16:25.960 --> 01:16:30.239
from her good-for-nothing storm god brother,
had won the day.

01:16:30.239 --> 01:16:37.370
A new Japanese unity was forged in the Mongol
fire.

01:16:37.370 --> 01:16:42.690
The island country of rival clans, competing
power centres, and riven factions that had

01:16:42.690 --> 01:16:48.239
disintegrated into banditry and disunity four
hundred years before, and only in the last

01:16:48.239 --> 01:16:55.239
century found an uneasy balance and firm borders
forged in civil war, had, under threat of

01:16:55.239 --> 01:17:02.090
foreign invasion, established a feeling that
it had never full enjoyed before, that of

01:17:02.090 --> 01:17:08.420
One Nation united against a common enemy.

01:17:08.420 --> 01:17:11.260
Japan would remain Japan.

